      {
        "id": 119809,
        "name": "Rabit",
        "username": "Rabit",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/rabit/{size}/7330_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-10-30T20:05:14.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"arenson9\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/48/1900_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> arenson9:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You say this, and yet it still doesn't seem right to me. The reason many cards are reapplied after a redirection is because the target has changed and therefore which cards are relevant has changed. Twist the Ether is relevant regardless of the target, so I don't see why its affects would be reconsidered. Yes I can see how tactically it could be very helpful, but so what?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>For me, it's an issue of consistency. If one adjustment (say the Nemesis bonus) has to be re-checked after redirection, they probably all should. Because Twist the Ether is an adjustment, it should be re-checked.</p>\n<p>That's just my thoughts, though.  <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/slightly_smiling_face.png?v=12\" title=\":slightly_smiling_face:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slightly_smiling_face:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>",
